    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically it's all about isolating those extra attributes into their own space so the main table stays clean. i guess i'm just worried about the complexity of managing all these extra joins and foreign keys. is there a point where over-normalizing actually makes things worse or just harder to maintain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct that isolating attributes keeps the main tables clean, but extreme normalization can indeed introduce complexity. If you normalize too deeply, you might create an overabundance of small tables requiring many joins for simple queries, which degrades performance. The goal is to reach the highest level of normal form necessary—usually 3NF or BCNF—without sacrificing practical query efficiency. A good balance minimizes joining overhead while still preventing update anomalies.",
      "source": "target",
